# Target C-II — odd exceptional faces force a two-window angular escape

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `9d7998d86d3e672ba4d1a7f627aadb2ed50aa219`  
**Status:** proof-only finite geometric lemma awaiting independent audit  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The existing pentagon regression correctly shows that a strict triangle-free unit
pentagon may approach the **three** projective lattice axes.  That blocks any uniform
"pentagon leaves the three-axis orbit" theorem.  It does not block a two-axis theorem.

There is in fact a simple parity obstruction: no closed odd equilateral polygon can
have all of its edge directions arbitrarily close to only two projective lines.  The
result is uniform in the angle between the two lines and gives an explicit pentagon
radius of

\[
\epsilon_5=2\arcsin(1/10)
=0.200334842323119\ldots
=11.4783409545\ldots ^\circ.
\]

This supplies exactly the kind of nonovercounting endpoint statement requested by the
older dense-pentagon reports, but only against **two** direction windows.  Three lattice
windows remain compatible with the known regression.

## 1. Odd-face two-window lemma

Let

\[
z_1,\ldots,z_d\in S^1,
\qquad
\sum_{i=1}^d z_i=0,
\]

be the oriented unit edge vectors of a closed equilateral polygon, with `d` odd.  Let
`[u],[v]` be any two projective directions.  Denote projective angular distance by
`dist_P`, taking values in `[0,pi/2]`.

Then

\[
\boxed{
\max_i\min\{\operatorname{dist}_P([z_i],[u]),
             \operatorname{dist}_P([z_i],[v])\}
\ge
\epsilon_d:=2\arcsin\!\left({1\over2d}\right).
}
\tag{OF.1}
\]

### Proof

Assume every `z_i` lies at projective angular distance `<epsilon` from one of the two
lines, where `epsilon<epsilon_d`.  For each edge choose

\[
w_i\in\{u,-u,v,-v\}
\]

so that its ordinary oriented angle from `z_i` is `<epsilon`.  Hence

\[
\|z_i-w_i\|<2\sin(\epsilon/2).
\tag{OF.2}
\]

Let `n_u` edges be assigned to the `u` line and `n_v=d-n_u` to the `v` line.  After
collecting signs,

\[
\sum_i w_i=a u+b v,
\tag{OF.3}
\]

where `a,b` are integers satisfying

\[
a\equiv n_u\pmod2,
\qquad
b\equiv n_v\pmod2.
\tag{OF.4}
\]

Because `d=n_u+n_v` is odd, `n_u,n_v` have opposite parity.  Therefore `a,b` have
opposite parity.

If one of `a,b` is zero, the other is a nonzero odd integer, so

\[
\|a u+b v\|\ge1.
\]

If both are nonzero, `|a|` and `|b|` are integers of opposite parity, hence
`||a|-|b||>=1`.  The reverse triangle inequality gives

\[
\|a u+b v\|
\ge\bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|
\ge1.
\tag{OF.5}
\]

On the other hand, polygon closure and (OF.2) give

\[
\left\|\sum_i w_i\right\|
=
\left\|\sum_i(w_i-z_i)\right\|
<2d\sin(\epsilon/2)
<1,
\tag{OF.6}
\]

contradicting (OF.5).  This proves (OF.1).

No convexity, unit-separation diagonal bound, or rhombic crossing theorem is needed;
only odd cardinality, unit edge lengths, and polygon closure enter the lemma.

## 2. Pentagon consequence

For `d=5`,

\[
\boxed{
\epsilon_5=2\arcsin(0.1)
=0.200334842323119\ldots\text{ radians}.
}
\tag{OF.7}
\]

Therefore for any two projective directions and any fixed

\[
0<\epsilon<\epsilon_5,
\]

every bounded pentagonal contact face contains at least one boundary edge whose
projective direction lies outside both `epsilon`-windows.

Every edge incident with a bounded nonquadrilateral contact face is a terminal
occurrence of its complete stopped train, and its edge direction is that train's
projective direction.  Hence if `p_5` bounded pentagons are present and
`N_off^bd(u,v;epsilon)` counts exceptional-face terminal occurrences belonging to
trains outside the two windows, then

\[
\boxed{
N_{off}^{bd}(u,v;\epsilon)\ge p_5.
}
\tag{OF.8}
\]

A stopped train has only two terminal incidences in total.  Thus if `T_off` is the
number of distinct stopped trains outside the same two windows,

\[
\boxed{
T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\ge {p_5\over2}.
}
\tag{OF.9}
\]

This is occurrence-safe: a single off-window train may pay two pentagons, one at each
end, but cannot pay a third.

## 3. Quantitative consequence for the old minimal-interface endpoint

The proved minimal-interface pentagon-density report gives, under its explicit
`Q_B/D -> 2` hypotheses,

\[
{p_5\over D}
\ge
2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-(\pi-2)-o(1)
=0.763032960138121\ldots-o(1).
\tag{OF.10}
\]

Therefore for **every** pair of projective directions and every fixed
`epsilon<epsilon_5`, that endpoint necessarily has

\[
\boxed{
{T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\over D}
\ge0.381516480069060\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{OF.11}
\]

So the dense pentagon fault network cannot be transported by two narrow train-direction
families plus `o(D)` exceptional trains.  Any proposed two-phase model of that endpoint
must leave at least `0.3815D` complete stopped trains outside its two phase windows.

This is a genuine strengthening of the older qualitative statement that the
`0.763D` pentagons require endpoint capacity: it identifies a uniform angular escape
and turns it into a distinct-train lower bound.

## 4. Compatibility with the known three-axis pentagon regression

There is no conflict with
`target_c_pentagon_lattice_phase_regression_2026-09-12.md`.  That construction makes
pentagon directions approach the three projective lines

\[
[0],\quad[\pi/3],\quad[2\pi/3].
\]

The present theorem forbids concentration near **two** lines only.  Indeed the
three-axis regression is evidence that the distinction is necessary: no analogous
positive escape radius can hold against three arbitrary projective windows.

This also explains how the lemma should be used.  It can close or sharpen branches
where an independent theorem has already reduced almost all stopped trains to two
narrow direction families.  It cannot by itself eliminate the global three-phase
lattice endpoint.

## 5. General odd-face version

For a bounded exceptional face of any odd degree `d>=5`, the same argument gives

\[
\epsilon_d=2\arcsin\!\left({1\over2d}\right).
\tag{OF.12}
\]

For reference,

\[
\epsilon_7=8.1920875163\ldots ^\circ,
\qquad
\epsilon_9=6.3694770734\ldots ^\circ.
\]

The radius decays like `1/d`, so the strongest application is precisely the
pentagon-dominated endpoint that existing Target-C reductions already isolate.

The parity and constants are regression-checked in
`research/verify_target_c_odd_face_two_window_gap.py`.

## 6. Scope

- This is a finite geometric lemma and does not assume global optimality.
- It counts projective **train directions** only through actual exceptional-face edge
  incidences; no source vertex is counted as a train.
- It does not assert that two global phase windows exist.  That must come from a
  separate phase-purity/stability theorem.
- It does not contradict the three-axis pentagon regression.
- It supplies no same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor by itself.
